{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["Pharaoh was furious with his courtiers — every 'Pharaoh' in the language of Egypt means 'king,' and so all the kings of Egypt are called thus. And those of the Philistines: Avimelekh — even in the days of David, when he altered his taste before Avimelekh. And those of Jerusalem: Malki-tzedek in the days of Yehoshua, Adoni-tzedek king of Shalem. And in the days of David — on my authority I assure you that he was Malki-tzedek king of Jerusalem. And those of Amalek: Agag. In the days of Moshe: וירם מאגג מלכו. And in the days of Shaul: Agag king of Amalek. So too here, Pharaoh is an expression of kingship, for in the king's own name one does not say before him [Pharaoh was furious with his courtiers] — rather, thus he said before him: My lord was furious with his courtiers. And likewise before us: אני פרעה — I alone shall be the king, as written: רק הכסא אגדל ממך. Therefore Yosef is called avrech — the father of the king. And in the book Lekach Tov he explained as I do."], :book :genesis, :voice :rashbam, :lang :en, :chapter 41, :notes {0 "The bracketed דוד appears to be a variant or marginal gloss; read with the following quotation it yields 'one does not say before him: [Pharaoh was furious…]' but the logic requires 'one does not use the name Pharaoh before Pharaoh himself,' so the bracketed word is retained as found."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:46:54.700948795Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}